Using an IAM Role with an IAM User/Group

1. Create an IAM Role (Example: jenkins-deployer).

2. Attach the required permission policies to the role, such as:
   - AmazonEC2ContainerRegistryPowerUser
   - Custom EKS Policy

3. If the role is for an EC2 instance (for example, Jenkins Agent), attach the IAM Role directly to the EC2 instance. The EC2 instance automatically receives all permissions attached to the role.

4. If an IAM User or IAM Group also needs the same permissions, do NOT attach the role directly because AWS does not support attaching roles to users or groups.

5. Instead, create a new IAM policy that allows the user or group to assume the role.

Example AssumeRole Policy:

{
  "Version": "2012-10-17",
  "Statement": [
    {
      "Effect": "Allow",
      "Action": "sts:AssumeRole",
      "Resource": "arn:aws:iam::<ACCOUNT_ID>:role/jenkins-deployer"
    }
  ]
}

6. Attach this AssumeRole policy to the required IAM User or IAM Group.

7. Update the Trust Policy of the jenkins-deployer role to trust the IAM User or IAM Group (or the AWS account, depending on your design), so that they are allowed to assume the role.

8. When the IAM User assumes the jenkins-deployer role, AWS provides temporary credentials, and the user automatically inherits all the permissions attached to the role (AmazonEC2ContainerRegistryPowerUser, Custom EKS Policy, etc.).

Summary:

For EC2:
IAM Role → Attach Permission Policies → Attach Role to EC2

For IAM Users/Groups:
IAM Role → Attach Permission Policies
IAM User/Group → Attach AssumeRole Policy (sts:AssumeRole) → Assume IAM Role → Get all Role Permissions

Note: IAM Roles cannot be attached directly to IAM Users or IAM Groups. They can only be assumed using the sts:AssumeRole permission.



=====================================================================================


Role → trusts John

John → allowed to assume Role

Role → has ECR/EKS permissions


IAM USER → ASSUME ROLE → EKS/ECR ACCESS

REAL-TIME EXAMPLE

Requirement:
John needs permission to use ECR and deploy to an EKS cluster.

Flow:

John (IAM User)
↓
sts:AssumeRole
↓
jenkins-deployer (IAM Role)
↓
ECR + EKS permissions


STEP 1 — CREATE THE IAM ROLE

Go to:

AWS Console
→ IAM
→ Roles
→ Create role

Choose: >>Trusted entity type: >>AWS account

Create the role : Role name: jenkins-deployer

Important:

The role has TWO types of policies:

1. Trust Policy
   → Who is allowed to assume the role?

2. Permission Policies
   → What can the role do after it is assumed?


STEP 2 — CONFIGURE THE ROLE TRUST POLICY

Go to:

IAM
→ Roles
→ jenkins-deployer
→ Trust relationships
→ Edit trust policy

Add John's IAM User ARN as the Principal.

Example:

{
  "Version": "2012-10-17",
  "Statement": [
    {
      "Effect": "Allow",
      "Principal": {
        "AWS": "arn:aws:iam::123456789012:user/john"
      },
      "Action": "sts:AssumeRole"
    }
  ]
}

Meaning:

jenkins-deployer trusts John.

John is allowed to assume this role from the role's trust-policy perspective.


STEP 3 — ATTACH PERMISSIONS TO THE ROLE

Go to:

IAM
→ Roles
→ jenkins-deployer
→ Permissions
→ Add permissions

Attach the permissions required by the job.

Example:

ECR permissions:
AmazonEC2ContainerRegistryPowerUser

EKS permissions:
Create a custom IAM policy containing only the AWS EKS API actions required.

Example:

{
  "Version": "2012-10-17",
  "Statement": [
    {
      "Effect": "Allow",
      "Action": [
        "eks:DescribeCluster",
        "eks:ListClusters",
        "eks:DescribeNodegroup",
        "eks:ListNodegroups"
      ],
      "Resource": "*"
    }
  ]
}

IMPORTANT:

These IAM permissions allow the role to call AWS EKS APIs.

They do NOT automatically mean the role can create/update Kubernetes Deployments or Pods.

For Kubernetes resource access, the IAM role must also be authorized in EKS using EKS Access Entry + EKS Access Policy, or Kubernetes RBAC, depending on the design.


STEP 4 — GO TO JOHN'S IAM USER

Go to:

IAM
→ Users
→ john
→ Permissions
→ Add permissions
→ Create inline policy

Create a policy allowing John to assume the role.

Example:

{
  "Version": "2012-10-17",
  "Statement": [
    {
      "Effect": "Allow",
      "Action": "sts:AssumeRole",
      "Resource": "arn:aws:iam::123456789012:role/jenkins-deployer"
    }
  ]
}

Meaning:

John is allowed to assume jenkins-deployer.


STEP 5 — BOTH SIDES MUST ALLOW ASSUMEROLE

Two checks happen:

ROLE TRUST POLICY:

jenkins-deployer
↓
Trusts John


JOHN'S PERMISSION POLICY:

John
↓
Allowed to sts:AssumeRole
↓
jenkins-deployer

Both sides must allow the operation.


STEP 6 — JOHN ASSUMES THE ROLE

John can run:

aws sts assume-role \
  --role-arn arn:aws:iam::123456789012:role/jenkins-deployer \
  --role-session-name john-session

AWS STS returns temporary credentials:

AccessKeyId
SecretAccessKey
SessionToken

John is now operating using temporary credentials for the jenkins-deployer role.


STEP 7 — JOHN GETS THE ROLE'S PERMISSIONS

After assuming the role:

John
↓
Temporary credentials
↓
jenkins-deployer
↓
Permissions attached to jenkins-deployer

For example:

ECR permissions
+
EKS AWS API permissions
+
EKS/Kubernetes permissions if separately configured


IMPORTANT CONCEPT:

The role is NOT permanently attached to John.

John temporarily assumes the role.

The permissions come from the role while John is using the temporary role credentials.


STEP 8 — EKS ACCESS

If John needs to deploy Kubernetes resources, the role must also be authorized to access the EKS cluster.

Recommended modern EKS approach:

EKS
→ Cluster
→ Access
→ Create access entry

Principal:

arn:aws:iam::123456789012:role/jenkins-deployer

Then associate the appropriate EKS Access Policy and scope it to the required namespace, if applicable.

Example:

jenkins-deployer
↓
EKS Access Entry
↓
EKS Access Policy
↓
dev namespace
↓
Kubernetes resources


FINAL FLOW

John
(IAM User)
↓
John has:
sts:AssumeRole permission
↓
STS AssumeRole
↓
jenkins-deployer
↓
Role has:
ECR permissions
+
